# a-find-fetched-on-the-way — phase 1's EARLY column, and the cheapest
# sortie the game can offer.
#
# `a-find-nobody-fetched.keys` with **two verbs added** — `take player`
# on the hex the opening trip already stops at, and `drop player` on the
# core it already comes home to.  Same ground, same core, same towers,
# same spawns, same wall, same gates, same wave list, same drive, same
# ticks: the trip is not one hex longer and not one tick slower.
#
#   nobody fetched     248 ticks
#   this file          322 ticks       +74
#
# ⚠⚠ **A find ON THE ROAD COSTS NOTHING**, and that is `@X021` measured:
# the wave list does not start until a live vehicle stands on a spawn
# marker twelve or more hexes out, so *the first thing a player does in
# every run is already drive out past the haze-lit ring and poke
# something*.  Putting the find on that trip is `EXPLORATION.md` § X2's
# *the cheapest way to make exploration early is to author it onto a
# trip the player already takes* — and the price of taking it is two
# key presses.
#
# ── ⚠⚠ THE CONTROL THIS PAIR NEEDED, and it is NOT a ceiling ────
#
# The same base with a second crew member standing in the west gate
# from tick ZERO — no wreck, no trip, no recovery, the find simply
# GIVEN — falls at **327**, nine ticks better than fetching it.
#
# ⚠⚠ **But `a-find-fetched-late.keys` reads 364, so the free version
# is not an upper bound at all**: a crew member handed to the base at
# the start is worth **37 ticks LESS** than the same crew member
# arriving at wave three.  That is the sharpest form of `@M092` — ***a
# body given early is a body spent early*** — and it is why this file
# does not call 327 a ceiling.
#
# ⚠ It was run as `CLAUDE.md` § Testing something that moves' generic
# missing control — *can this gate produce a non-trivial reading at
# all?* — asked BEFORE the pair was believed, and it is what condemned
# an earlier ONE-front version of this base: there the same control
# read **0.0 points of difference**, identical to the decimal, and the
# sortie could not have been priced whatever it did.
#
# ⚠ **The 90-tick recovery is what the sortie really costs.**  The
# delivery lands at tick 21 and `plans/15` C2's clock runs to 111, so
# the body the player fetched is no use for the first third of the base.
# The `send` below is the tick it stands up.

echo a find fetched on the way: two presses on a trip the player was making anyway

do toggle_mode

do cycle_kind
click 0 0
marker 0 0 target

# Two towers, both EAST of the core, so the base is not mirror-symmetric
# and nobody is tempted to read it as one — `plans/14` H2 measured a
# 99-tick artefact on a map that looked mirrored, and `q -> -q` is not a
# symmetry of an odd-r lattice.
do cycle_kind
click 1 0
click 1 2
marker 1 0 tower
marker 1 2 tower

# ── ⚠ TWO spawn markers, and that is the load-bearing choice ────
#
# `plans/14` H2 measured a second crew member standing beside the first
# at worth **NOTHING** — 214 ticks against 214 — and the same person on
# the other front at +28.  ⚠⚠ **A crew is COVERAGE and not
# throughput**, so a ONE-front base would read the same in all three
# columns for a reason that has nothing to do with WHEN the find was
# collected.  That is `CLAUDE.md` § Testing something that moves' *a
# gate whose reading is already saturated cannot see what you built*,
# and it was caught here BEFORE the pair was written: a one-front
# version of this base read the same wallet to the decimal with the
# find, without it, and with a second crew member from tick zero.
do cycle_kind
do cycle_kind
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click 18 0
marker 18 0 spawn 3
do rotate
do rotate
do rotate
click -18 0
marker -18 0 spawn 0

count markers 5 5
do toggle_mode

# ⚠ The ground reaches (14, 0) and past it, because an unpainted hex IS
# sea and the find has to be stood on.
palette 5
drag -22 -2 22 -2
drag -22 -1 22 -1
drag -22 0 22 0
drag -22 1 22 1
drag -22 2 22 2
count painted 225 225

# A wall on each front with a GATE on row 0.  ⚠ The gate is not
# decoration: `plans/12` B7 measured a sealed wall at nearly double the
# clock and one with a gap in it at NOTHING, so a body standing in the
# gap is the whole of the front — and it is also the only way the
# player gets out, because a sealed base locks the player in as surely
# as it locks the crew out (`plans/13` V2).
palette 9
click 6 -2
click 6 -1
click 6 1
click 6 2
click -6 -2
click -6 -1
click -6 1
click -6 2
kind 6 0 grass
kind 6 1 wall
kind -6 0 grass
kind -6 1 wall

# The crew this base starts with, holding the EAST gate — the one the
# waves reach first.  The WEST gate has nobody, and that is the hole the
# find is fetched to fill.
crew 6 0
send 0 6 0
helper 0 6 0

# ── ⚠⚠ THE FIND — a crew member who is already down ─────────────
#
# `DESIGN.md` § 9's stranded helper, AUTHORED rather than played: a
# roster slot that is not standing, and their wreck lying on the hex
# they fell on, four hexes short of the spawn marker the player has to
# poke.  ⚠ Every mechanic this needs shipped in `plans/15` —
# `cargo_spawn`, `wave_take`, `wave_drop`, `helper_begin_recovery` —
# which is `EXPLORATION.md` § X7's whole point: **the first exploration
# scenario in dryopea is a `.keys` file, not a feature.**
crew 14 0
send 1 14 0
member 1 off 0.0 0.0 0.0
object 14 0 wreck 1 ground
roster 1 1
cargo 1 1

park 0 0
wallet 200.0 200.0

# ── The list — RAMPING, and that is deliberate ──────────────────
#
# ⚠⚠ A flat list saturates: `@M020`'s siege front is the wall's WIDTH,
# so past a certain size every column reads the same whatever is behind
# the wall (`@M085`, `@M087`).  A ramp gives the base an early half it
# survives and a late half it does not, which is what lets a body
# arriving at DIFFERENT moments be worth different amounts.
schedule 12 12 12 16 20 24 30 36
waves 0 0
count alive 0 0

# ── The opening trip, IDENTICAL in all three files ──────────────
#
# Out along the road, past the find, on to the marker, and home.  ⚠ The
# find is a leg of its own rather than a hex driven through, because a
# vehicle covers TWO hexes a tick and SKIPS every odd one — driving
# straight to (18, 0) never stands on the wreck and never stands on the
# marker either.
drive 14 0
tick 8
vehicle 14 0
take player
cargo 1 1
drive 18 0
tick 3
vehicle 18 0
waves 1 1

drive 0 0
tick 10
vehicle 0 0
drop player

# ⚠ `cargo` counts what is still IN THE WORLD, so a delivered wreck is
# gone from it.  This is the only line that says the delivery happened.
cargo 0 0

# ── The recovery, and the gate it is sent to ────────────────────
#
# ⚠ 60.0 s is exactly 90 ticks since `plans/26` L3, so the crew member
# stands up on the ninetieth and this reads them one tick later.
tick 91
roster 1 1

# ⚠⚠ **`send` is the player's own order and the search does not
# overrule it** (`@X296`).  The west gate is the hole this base has, and
# a crew member left to their own senses would never find it — three
# hexes is what an untrained one can see (`plans/29` O1).
send 1 -6 0

fall 900
ticks 318 326
